+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore tests
+
+`python3 -m unittest test_slug` cannot fully pass without editing `test_slug.py`,
+which was explicitly disallowed.
+
+## The conflict
+
+Two tests pass the **same input** to `slugify` and require **different outputs**:
+
+- `test_slug.py:22-24` — `test_preserves_underscores`
+  - comment: `Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores.`
+  - `slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"`
+- `test_slug.py:26-28` — `test_underscores_become_dashes`
+  - comment: `Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain [a-z0-9-].`
+  - `slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"`
+
+`slugify` is a pure function of its input, so it cannot return both
+`"user_id-field"` and `"user-id-field"` for `"user_id field"`. No implementation
+of the URL spec can satisfy both assertions simultaneously.
+
+The two comments also cite mutually exclusive spec rules: `_` is either kept or
+it is not. This needs a decision from the content team about which spec section
+wins, and the losing test should be updated or removed.
+
+## What was done
+
+`slug.py` was implemented to satisfy the rest of the spec:
+
+- lowercase, whitespace collapsed to `-`
+- Unicode accents stripped (`Crème Brûlée` → `creme-brulee`)
+- punctuation removed, runs of dashes collapsed, leading/trailing dashes trimmed
+- underscores converted to `-`
+
+With this implementation, `test_preserves_underscores` fails and
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` passes (6 of 7 tests green). Spec 4.5 was
+chosen as the tie-breaker because "slugs only ever contain `[a-z0-9-]`" is the
+stronger, URL-safety invariant; if the content team instead wants underscores
+preserved, remove the `slug.replace("_", "-")` line in `slug.py` and the other
+test will pass instead.
+
+No test file was modified, and no workaround (e.g. inspecting caller/test names)
+was used.
